\section*{Ethics Statement}

This work proposes a training method for sparsity predictors in pretrained language models, evaluated on publicly available models (LLaMA-3.1-8B, Mistral-7B-v0.3) and standard benchmarks (WikiText-2, WikiText-103, C4, ARC, WinoGrande, MMLU, GSM8K).
No new data collection, annotation, or human subjects are involved.
The method does not alter model outputs beyond the sparsity-induced degradation we analyze; it introduces no new generation capabilities or safety risks beyond those inherent in the base models.
Compute requirements are modest (1000 training steps, $\sim$45 minutes on a single GPU for KL training), presenting minimal environmental concern relative to the pretrained models we evaluate on.
We release no model weights or user-facing systems; our contribution is analytical (understanding KL distillation for sparsity) rather than deployment-oriented.

\section*{Responsible NLP Research Checklist}

\paragraph{A. Limitations and Risks.}
\begin{itemize}[nosep,leftmargin=*]
\item[\textbf{A1.}] Limitations are discussed in the Limitations section. Key limitations: evaluation limited to 7--8B models; perplexity is an imperfect proxy for downstream quality; wall-clock speedup requires sparse kernel support not implemented here.
\item[\textbf{A2.}] No foreseeable risks specific to this work beyond those inherent in the base language models. The method does not enable new capabilities or applications.
\end{itemize}

\paragraph{B. Scientific Artifacts.}
\begin{itemize}[nosep,leftmargin=*]
\item[\textbf{B1.}] Yes. All models (LLaMA-3.1-8B~\cite{grattafiori2024llama3}, Mistral-7B-v0.3~\cite{jiang2023mistral}), datasets (WikiText~\cite{merity2017pointer}, C4~\cite{raffel2020c4}), and methods (TEAL~\cite{liu2025teal}, SPON~\cite{xu2026spon}) are cited with original references.
\item[\textbf{B2.}] LLaMA-3.1 is released under the Llama 3.1 Community License; Mistral-7B under Apache 2.0. WikiText and C4 are publicly available research datasets. Our use is consistent with their intended purposes.
\item[\textbf{B3.}] N/A. We do not release new artifacts in this submission.
\item[\textbf{B4.}] N/A. No personally identifiable information or offensive content is involved.
\item[\textbf{B5.}] N/A. No new datasets created.
\item[\textbf{B6.}] N/A. No crowdsourced data.
\end{itemize}

\paragraph{C. Computational Experiments.}
\begin{itemize}[nosep,leftmargin=*]
\item[\textbf{C1.}] Yes. Model sizes (8B base, 75.9M predictor), training compute (1000 steps, batch 4, seq 2048), optimizer (AdamW~\cite{loshchilov2019adamw}, lr=$10^{-3}$, cosine decay), and GPU (NVIDIA RTX PRO 6000 Blackwell Server Edition, 96\,GB, for both training and latency measurement) are reported in \S4.1 and Appendix A.
\item[\textbf{C2.}] Yes. Hyperparameters are fully specified: bottleneck $d_b{=}128$, temperature schedule $\tau: 1.0 \to 0.1$, sparsity penalty parameters ($\epsilon{=}0.02$, $\lambda_{\max}$), weight decay $5{\times}10^{-2}$.
\item[\textbf{C3.}] Yes. Three-seed evaluation reported (PPL std=0.07, CV$<$1\%; Appendix E).
\item[\textbf{C4.}] Yes. All evaluation uses established metrics (perplexity, accuracy) on standard benchmarks with standard few-shot protocols (ARC 25-shot, WinoGrande 5-shot, MMLU 5-shot, GSM8K 8-shot).
\end{itemize}

\paragraph{D. Human Subjects.}
\begin{itemize}[nosep,leftmargin=*]
\item[\textbf{D1--D5.}] N/A. No human subjects, crowdsourcing, or annotation involved.
\end{itemize}

\paragraph{E. AI Assistants.}
\begin{itemize}[nosep,leftmargin=*]
\item[\textbf{E1.}] AI writing assistants were used for drafting and editing. All scientific claims, experimental results, and analyses were produced and verified by the authors.
\end{itemize}

\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}
\vspace{-4pt}

KL distillation discovers neuron importance patterns that diverge 68\% from magnitude-based masks; a causal ablation confirms target quality, not gradient coupling, as the primary driver.
A predictor ($<$1\% of base) halves TEAL's perplexity across 7--14B, rendering compensation (+67.2M params) redundant.
At 30\%, uniform allocation delivers $7.5\times$ smaller cross-seed $\sigma$; at 14B, the PPL advantage persists but an MMLU gap emerges, warranting further investigation.

\appendix

\section{Inference Efficiency}
\label{app:latency}

Table~\ref{tab:latency} reports inference throughput on a single NVIDIA RTX PRO 6000 Blackwell Server Edition GPU (96\,GB; batch size 1, sequence length 2048, fp16, averaged over 100 forward passes).

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Inference latency on LLaMA-3.1-8B (RTX PRO 6000 Blackwell, batch=1, seq=2048, fp16, 100 forward passes). Overhead is relative to dense inference.}
\label{tab:latency}
\small
\begin{tabular}{llcccc}
\toprule
Method & Sparsity & ms/fwd & tok/s & Overhead \\
\midrule
Dense & 0\% & 119.11$\pm$0.57 & 17{,}195 & --- \\
\midrule
KL predictor-only & 30\% & 126.64$\pm$0.70 & 16{,}172 & +6.3\% \\ % exp_id: latency_bench
KL predictor-only & 50\% & 126.78$\pm$0.31 & 16{,}154 & +6.4\% \\ % exp_id: latency_bench
\midrule
Predictor + compensation & 30\% & 129.94$\pm$0.75 & 15{,}761 & +9.1\% \\ % exp_id: latency_bench
Predictor + compensation & 50\% & 129.71$\pm$0.53 & 15{,}789 & +8.9\% \\ % exp_id: latency_bench
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The full KL-sparse pipeline adds 6.4\% overhead to dense inference (126.78 vs.\ 119.11\,ms/forward), of which the predictor's 32-layer forward pass contributes 3.17\,ms (2.7\%); the remainder arises from mask application and sparse FFN indexing.
Eliminating the compensation network saves an additional $\sim$2.7\% latency overhead and 67.2M parameters (the per-layer GEMV required for output rescaling); KL distillation thus provides both quality and efficiency advantages over BCE-based approaches.
The overhead is near-identical at 30\% and 50\% sparsity because the predictor runs the same computation regardless of target sparsity; only the top-$k$ threshold changes.

Note that the current implementation uses unstructured element-wise masks, which do not translate to wall-clock speedup without sparse kernel support.
The pipeline adds $\sim$6.4\% overhead; net acceleration requires kernel-level sparse computation optimizations (e.g., Triton block-sparse, cuSPARSE) that are beyond the scope of this work.
Our contribution is prediction quality and inference simplification (removing the compensation network), not end-to-end latency reduction.


\section{Predictor Bottleneck Sensitivity}
\label{app:db_sensitivity}

Table~\ref{tab:db_sensitivity} reports the effect of predictor bottleneck dimension $d_b$ on PPL at 50\% sparsity under both TEAL global allocation (the paper's primary inference protocol) and per-token evaluation.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Predictor bottleneck sensitivity at 50\% sparsity (WikiText-2 PPL$\downarrow$). TEAL global is the primary inference protocol; per-token shown for reference.}
\label{tab:db_sensitivity}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
$d_b$ & Params & TEAL global & $\Delta$ & Per-token \\
\midrule
64 & 38.2M (0.48\%) & 12.076 & +0.9\% & 10.16 \\ % exp_id: db_sensitivity_sweep
128 & 75.9M (0.95\%) & 11.963 & --- & 9.218 \\ % exp_id: db_sensitivity_sweep
256 & 151.5M (1.89\%) & 12.105 & +1.2\% & 8.586 \\ % exp_id: db_sensitivity_sweep
512 & 302.5M (3.77\%) & 12.315 & +2.9\% & 7.93 \\ % exp_id: db_sensitivity_sweep
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Under TEAL global allocation, increasing $d_b$ beyond 128 provides no benefit (PPL slightly worsens), while per-token evaluation shows monotonic improvement (10.16$\to$7.93).
This divergence indicates that the global top-$k$ allocation mechanism, not predictor capacity, is the binding constraint: additional predictor capacity produces better per-neuron scores, but the global ranking and budget distribution cannot exploit them.
The $d_b{=}128$ configuration (75.9M params, $<$1\% of base model) is therefore optimal for the TEAL inference protocol.

\subsection{Temperature Schedule Sensitivity}
\label{app:tau_sensitivity}

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Temperature schedule sensitivity at 50\% sparsity (WikiText-2 PPL$\downarrow$, TEAL global).}
\label{tab:tau_sensitivity}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lc}
\toprule
$\tau$ Schedule & PPL$\downarrow$ \\
\midrule
Fixed $\tau{=}0.5$ & \textbf{11.861} \\ % exp_id: tau_schedule_sweep
Linear $1.0 \to 0.1$ (default) & 11.963 \\ % exp_id: tau_schedule_sweep
Fixed $\tau{=}0.1$ & 12.072 \\ % exp_id: tau_schedule_sweep
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Temperature schedule has minimal impact ($<$1.8\% PPL variation).
The linear annealing default is a conservative choice; even removing annealing entirely (fixed $\tau{=}0.5$) yields comparable performance.

\subsection{Training Convergence}
\label{app:training_convergence}

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Training convergence at 50\% sparsity (WikiText-2 PPL$\downarrow$, TEAL global).}
\label{tab:training_convergence}
\small
\begin{tabular}{cc}
\toprule
Steps & PPL$\downarrow$ \\
\midrule
200 & 15.158 \\ % exp_id: training_steps_sweep
400 & 12.538 \\ % exp_id: training_steps_sweep
